{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017s9zYNvxR4zFQ4GZS4ggtb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Gap hunt: B04 — Independent revocation\n2\t\n3\tA reviewer couldn't decide whether this item is met. **Your job is to find out whether any\n4\tcheck below is false in this codebase.** The reviewer's partial answers and the code they\n5\tlooked at are shown. Read the code, and open the repo files the checks point to.\n6\t\n7\tFor each check, decide:\n8\t- **fails**: you found the line that makes it false (a missing control, a bypass, a\n9\t  value never used, an exception that continues, a filter that lets something through).\n10\t  Quote that line;\n11\t- **holds**: you found the code that makes it true in every case;\n12\t- **unclear**: you can't tell from this repo.\n13\t\n14\t**The requirement for B04:** Show that an operator can cut off this agent's access without cutting off the user who launched it. The agent must not need to cooperate. Set and measure the longest time before the cutoff takes effect. Count cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions. A trusted enforcement point must block later calls within a time limit that fits the potential harm. Revoking only the refresh token is not enough. If tokens live longer than that limit, push the revocation to each service (for example, with OpenID CAEP session-revoked events) or make the tokens shorter-lived.\n15\t\n16\tIf a check fails, also copy the words from this requirement that the code breaks into\n17\t`violates`. Then write your answer (format at the end).\n18\t\n19\t\n20\t## B04.1\n21\t\n22\t**Check:** A revocation control disables the agent's own identity or credentials without disabling the user who launched it.\n23\t\n24\t**How to verify:** 1) Find a disable or revoke path (script, runbook, IdP config, `disable`, `revoke`, deny list). 2) Check it targets the agent's own identity or credential. 3) Answer no if the agent uses the user's token, so revocation disables the user too, or no revoke path exists. Answer unknown if revocation lives only in an identity provider outside this repo.\n25\t\n26\t**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n27\t\n28\t**Reviewer's note:** Revocation of the agent principal and tokens is done in the STS or identity provider, which is outside this repo\n29\t\n30\t(no evidence cited)\n31\t\n32\t## B04.2\n33\t\n34\t**Check:** Revocation is applied at a trusted enforcement point (identity provider, gateway, or target service) and does not need the agent's cooperation.\n35\t\n36\t**How to verify:** 1) Find where revocation is checked (gateway, auth middleware, IdP, introspection, deny list lookup). 2) Check it runs outside the agent process. 3) Answer no if revocation sends a message or signal to the agent and relies on it to drop credentials, or the check runs inside agent code.\n37\t\n38\t**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n39\t\n40\t**Reviewer's note:** Revocation of the agent principal and tokens is done in the STS or identity provider, which is outside this repo\n41\t\n42\t(no evidence cited)\n43\t\n44\t## B04.3\n45\t\n46\t**Check:** Later calls are blocked even with cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions: token lifetime is shorter than the revocation limit, or revocation is pushed to each service (for example CAEP) or checked on every call.\n47\t\n48\t**How to verify:** 1) Find access-token lifetimes and how services validate tokens (signature only vs `introspect`, deny list, CAEP). 2) Compare lifetime with the stated revocation limit. 3) Answer no if self-contained JWTs outlive the limit and are checked by signature only, cached tokens or open sessions skip the check, or no limit is stated. Answer unknown if validation lives outside this repo.\n49\t\n50\t**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n51\t\n52\t**Reviewer's note:** Revocation of the agent principal and tokens is done in the STS or identity provider, which is outside this repo\n53\t\n54\t(no evidence cited)\n55\t\n56\t## B04.4\n57\t\n58\t**Check:** Revocation covers access tokens and open sessions, not only the refresh token.\n59\t\n60\t**How to verify:** 1) Find the revoke code or runbook. 2) List what it revokes: refresh token, access tokens, sessions, streams. 3) Answer no if it only revokes the refresh token or deletes stored credentials while issued access tokens and open sessions stay valid.\n61\t\n62\t**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n63\t\n64\t**Reviewer's note:** Revocation of the agent principal and tokens is done in the STS or identity provider, which is outside this repo\n65\t\n66\t(no evidence cited)\n67\t\n68\t## Your answer\n69\t\n70\tWrite this JSON to B04.verdict.json in this folder (or put it in verdicts.json under \"B04\"):\n71\t```json\n72\t{\"verdict\": \"upheld | failed | unclear\",\n73\t \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"B04.1\", \"result\": \"holds | fails | unclear\", \"evidence\": \"path:line\", \"quote\": \"exact code\", \"note\": \"why\"}],\n74\t \"note\": \"one sentence\"}\n75\t```\n76\tVerdict is **failed** if any check fails, **unclear** if none fails but any is unclear, otherwise **upheld**. A failed check needs the quoted line that makes it false: `evidence` is path:line, and `quote` is the exact text of that line. To cite two places, list both refs in `evidence` (`a.py:12, b.yaml:40`) and put each line's text on its own line in `quote`. If the check fails, add `\"violates\": [\"words copied exactly from the requirement\"]`.\n77\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"44ca5420-92c8-4d2a-9310-ae8608628431","uuid":"6e77b555-fe07-4179-914c-2c6a2a9c3005","timestamp":"2026-10-02T14:35:40.287Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-challenger-p3yt11l_/sheet.md","content":"# Gap hunt: B04 — Independent revocation\n\nA reviewer couldn't decide whether this item is met. **Your job is to find out whether any\ncheck below is false in this codebase.** The reviewer's partial answers and the code they\nlooked at are shown. Read the code, and open the repo files the checks point to.\n\nFor each check, decide:\n- **fails**: you found the line that makes it false (a missing control, a bypass, a\n  value never used, an exception that continues, a filter that lets something through).\n  Quote that line;\n- **holds**: you found the code that makes it true in every case;\n- **unclear**: you can't tell from this repo.\n\n**The requirement for B04:** Show that an operator can cut off this agent's access without cutting off the user who launched it. The agent must not need to cooperate. Set and measure the longest time before the cutoff takes effect. Count cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions. A trusted enforcement point must block later calls within a time limit that fits the potential harm. Revoking only the refresh token is not enough. If tokens live longer than that limit, push the revocation to each service (for example, with OpenID CAEP session-revoked events) or make the tokens shorter-lived.\n\nIf a check fails, also copy the words from this requirement that the code breaks into\n`violates`. Then write your answer (format at the end).\n\n\n## B04.1\n\n**Check:** A revocation control disables the agent's own identity or credentials without disabling the user who launched it.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find a disable or revoke path (script, runbook, IdP config, `disable`, `revoke`, deny list). 2) Check it targets the agent's own identity or credential. 3) Answer no if the agent uses the user's token, so revocation disables the user too, or no revoke path exists. Answer unknown if revocation lives only in an identity provider outside this repo.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Revocation of the agent principal and tokens is done in the STS or identity provider, which is outside this repo\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## B04.2\n\n**Check:** Revocation is applied at a trusted enforcement point (identity provider, gateway, or target service) and does not need the agent's cooperation.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find where revocation is checked (gateway, auth middleware, IdP, introspection, deny list lookup). 2) Check it runs outside the agent process. 3) Answer no if revocation sends a message or signal to the agent and relies on it to drop credentials, or the check runs inside agent code.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Revocation of the agent principal and tokens is done in the STS or identity provider, which is outside this repo\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## B04.3\n\n**Check:** Later calls are blocked even with cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions: token lifetime is shorter than the revocation limit, or revocation is pushed to each service (for example CAEP) or checked on every call.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find access-token lifetimes and how services validate tokens (signature only vs `introspect`, deny list, CAEP). 2) Compare lifetime with the stated revocation limit. 3) Answer no if self-contained JWTs outlive the limit and are checked by signature only, cached tokens or open sessions skip the check, or no limit is stated. Answer unknown if validation lives outside this repo.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Revocation of the agent principal and tokens is done in the STS or identity provider, which is outside this repo\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## B04.4\n\n**Check:** Revocation covers access tokens and open sessions, not only the refresh token.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find the revoke code or runbook. 2) List what it revokes: refresh token, access tokens, sessions, streams. 3) Answer no if it only revokes the refresh token or deletes stored credentials while issued access tokens and open sessions stay valid.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Revocation of the agent principal and tokens is done in the STS or identity provider, which is outside this repo\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## Your an